  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2)/2, steeper along x (across the view). The surface is drawn at half the loss height. Without noise, x overshoots for learning rates above 1/2.4 ≈ 0.42 and the swings grow above 2/2.4 ≈ 0.83.",
    "Step = -rate × (slope + noise). Each noise component is Gaussian with spread 1.2·sqrt((64−B)/(63B)): 1.2 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. The noise continues in sequence across new runs, and Reset replays it from the start.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 4 times their real length, using one scale for both. Purple: this step against the noisy estimate. Dark: the step the true full-data slope would give. An arrow is hidden when the run has ended.",
    "A run finishes after 40 steps. It is diverged when the ball leaves the drawn bowl (|x| or |z| over 2) or the loss passes 6. An off-bowl ball and path are drawn at the rim, and the Loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "The trail holds the start plus up to 40 landing points, and so does the ghost. The ball takes 5 steps per second at 1× speed."
  ]
}